**Performance problem:** Line 5 (`out += p`) causes **avoidable reallocations from repeated appends**. Each `+=` may trigger a reallocation as `out` grows, copying all existing characters each time — O(n) reallocations, O(n²) total copy cost.

**Idiomatic fix:** Compute the total size upfront and call `out.reserve(total_size)` before the loop, so the string allocates exactly the needed capacity once.

```cpp
std::string join(const std::vector<std::string>& parts) {
  std::string out;
  for (const auto& p : parts) out.reserve(out.size() + p.size());  // or pre-sum
  for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;
  return out;
}
```

Or more cleanly, sum sizes first:

```cpp
std::string join(const std::vector<std::string>& parts) {
  std::string out;
  for (const auto& p : parts) out.reserve(out.size() + p.size());
  for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;
  return out;
}
```

The key insight: `reserve` when the total size is known (or can be computed) eliminates all intermediate reallocations.

